          %h2#what_is_homotopy_theory What is homotopy theory?
          %h2#intro Is homotopy theory a part of algebraic topology?
          %p
            On the relation between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            and (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"}> algebraic
            )
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
          %p
            In one of the first textbooks on
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            %em
              %a{:href => "homotopy+theory#Hilton53"} An introduction to homotopy theory
            by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+J.+Hilton"} Peter J. Hilton
            (1953), one reads:
          %blockquote
            %p
              Since the introduction of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups"} homotopy groups
              by
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hurewicz"} Hurewicz
              in 1935,
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
              has been occupying an increasingly prominent place in the field of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
              suitable for those who wish to undertake research in the subject and for those who wish to be in a position to understand the modern techniques and results.
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sze-Tsen+Hu"} Sze-Tsen Hu
            writes in
            %em
              %a{:href => "homotopy+theory#SzeTsen59"} Homotopy Theory
            (1959):
          %blockquote
            %p
              The recognition of the branch of mathematics now called
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
              took place in the few years after the introduction of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups"} homotopy groups
              by
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Witold+Hurewicz"} Witold Hurewicz
              in 1935. Since then, with numerous advances made by various workers, it has been playing an increasingly important role in the expanding field of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/P.+J.+Hilton"} P. J. Hilton
              entitled “
              %a{:href => "homotopy+theory#Hilton53"}> An Introduction to Homotopy Theory
              ”.
          %p
            Thus, at the time
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            was clearly identified as a part of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
          %p
            The 1995
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Handbook+of+Algebraic+Topology"} Handbook of Algebraic Topology
            (edited by
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ioan+M.+James"} Ioan M. James
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
          %p
            However, the attitudes have changed since then.
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haynes+Miller"} Haynes Miller
            writes in the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Handbook+of+Homotopy+Theory"} Handbook of Homotopy Theory
            (2019):
          %blockquote
            %p
              This volume may be regarded as a successor to the “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Handbook+of+Algebraic+Topology"}> Handbook of Algebraic Topology
              ,” edited by
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ioan+James"} Ioan James
              and published a quarter of a century ago. In calling it the “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Handbook+of+Homotopy+Theory"}> Handbook of Homotopy Theory
              ,” I am recognizing that the discipline has expanded and deepened, and traditional questions of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
              proper.
          %p#BarwickOnHomotopyTheory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Clark+Barwick"} Clark Barwick
            writes in
            %em
              %a{:href => "https://www.maths.ed.ac.uk/~cbarwick/papers/future.pdf"} The future of homotopy theory
            (2018):
          %blockquote
            %p
              Neither our subject nor its interaction with other areas of inquiry is widely understood. Some of us
              %a{:href => "#BarwickFootnote4"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mrow
                        %mn 4
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {}^4
              call ourselves
              = succeed "," do
                %em algebraic topologists
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
              %a{:href => "#BarwickFootnote5"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mrow
                        %mn 5
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {}^5
              Today, the praxis of
              = succeed "theory" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
              no more often than it does with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arithmetic+geometry"} arithmetic geometry
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation+theory"} representation theory
              are growing rapidly.
              %em Homotopy theory is not a branch of topology.
              This is important, because as long as homotopy theory is classified under the umbrella of topology, there will be errors of judgement in who is considered competent to judge our work; the results of this at journals, on the job market, and in funding is real and lasting.
            %p#BarwickFootnote4
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mrow
                      %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {}^4
              not me
            %p#BarwickFootnote5
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mrow
                      %mn 5
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {}^5
              I think of homotopy theory as an enrichment of the notion of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equality
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stuff%2C+structure%2C+property"} structure over properties
          %h2#what_objects_does_homotopy_theory_study What objects does homotopy theory study?
          %p
            We list some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematical+objects"} mathematical objects
            that are undoubtedly studied by
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The first object is known under various names:
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} ∞-groupoid
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"} (homotopy) type
                %a{:href => "#spaces"} section on “spaces”
                for an explanation of why so many different names are used for (essentially) the same concept. These objects can be modeled by
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
                up to
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+weak+equivalence"} simplicial weak equivalence
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
                up to
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+homotopy+equivalence"} weak homotopy equivalence
                %a{:href => "#models"} section on models
                for more details about models.
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Spectra"} Spectra
                or
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+types"} stable homotopy types
              %p
                Specific
                %a{:href => "#models"} models
                include
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequential+spectra"} sequential spectra
                or
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+spectra"} symmetric spectra
                (valued in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
                or
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orthogonal+spectra"} orthogonal spectra
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reduced+excisive+functors"} reduced excisive functors
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-categories"}> ∞-categories
                , also known as
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theories"} homotopy theories
              %p
                Specific
                %a{:href => "#models"} models
                include
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+categories"} relative categories
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasicategories"} quasicategories
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+categories"} simplicial categories
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Segal+categories"} Segal categories
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+Segal+spaces"} complete Segal spaces
                %a{:href => "#infinity"} section on ∞-categories
                for more information.
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-categories"}> (∞,n)-categories
                , which can be
                %a{:href => "#models"} modeled
                by
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/globular+n-fold+complete+Segal+spaces"} globular n-fold complete Segal spaces
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Theta-n+spaces"} Theta-n spaces
          %h2#what_objects_does_algebraic_topology_study What objects does algebraic topology study?
          %p
            As already explained in the
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#intro"} introduction
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            from
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
            is once the separation happens.
          %p
            We list some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematical+objects"} mathematical objects
            that are undoubtedly studied by
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifolds"}> manifolds
                , as studied in
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surgery+theory"} surgery theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bordism+theory"} bordism theory
              %p
                These include
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+manifolds"} topological manifolds
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/PL-manifolds"} PL-manifolds
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifolds"} smooth manifolds
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knots"}> knots
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangles"} tangles
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surfaces"} surfaces
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifolds"} manifolds
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groups"} Lie groups
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/characteristic+classes"} characteristic classes
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polyhedra"}> polyhedra
                \.
          %h2#spaces What are “spaces” in homotopy theory?
          %p
            These are some of the first and most important objects introduced in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
          %p This is witnessed by the many names given to them, which all describe the same concept but have a slightly different emphasis:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoid"}> ∞-groupoid
              , which emphasizes the categorical point of view;
            %li
              (homotopy) space, which emphasizes the original
              %a{:href => "#models"} model
              using
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
              up to
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+homotopy+equivalence"} weak homotopy equivalence
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"}> homotopy type
              , which emphasizes that objects in a
              %a{:href => "#models"} model
              are only considered up to
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+homotopy+equivalence"} weak homotopy equivalence
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"}> type
              , which alludes to
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
          %p
            Yet even this large variety of viewpoints is sometimes deemed insufficient, which leads mathematicians to introduce further names for such objects, such as “anima” used by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dustin+Clausen"} Dustin Clausen
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Scholze"} Peter Scholze
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertical+categorification"} vertical categorification
          %p
            We emphasize that these objects (except for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types"} types
            in
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalence"} weak equivalence
            %em not
            isomorphism. For example, when using
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            as
            %a{:href => "#models"} models
            for (homotopy) spaces, we are
            %em not
            interested in invariants under
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphisms"} homeomorphisms
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+homotopy+equivalences"} weak homotopy equivalences
            %a{:href => "#models"} section on models
            for more details.
          %h2#models What is a model in homotopy theory?
          %p
            Informal ideas of objects studied by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            may emerge as an alternative in the future.
          %p
            This process introduces artifacts that are not present in the original informal idea and have no meaning in that context. As an example, suppose we present a (homotopy) space using a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            of 0-simplices or
            = succeed "-simplices" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n\ge0
            is a piece of data specific to our chosen presentation. Another presentation may have a set of 0-simplices of different cardinality, say. On the other hand, if we take the set of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+components"} connected components
            of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+weak+equivalences"} simplicial weak equivalences
          %p
            Thus
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
            up to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+weak+equivalences"} simplicial weak equivalences
            %em model
            (homotopy) spaces.
          %p
            One common way to encode models is to organize them in a
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+category"} relative category
          %h2#why_do_we_need_models_in_homotopy_theory Why do we need models in homotopy theory?
          %p The short answer is: we do not know how to work without models.
          %p
            A good example is the notion of an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Platonic"} Platonic
            level, and whose “shadows” (using Plato’s terminology) in the world of rigorous mathematics have been exhibited as various models of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+categories"} relative categories
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasicategories"} quasicategories
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Segal+categories"} Segal categories
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+Segal+spaces"} complete Segal spaces
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+categories"} simplicial categories
          %p
            However, the concept of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
            per se resists formalization in a satisfactory way.
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Homotopy+type+theory"} Homotopy type theory
            may succeed one day on this path, but so far no working definition has been exhibited yet.
          %h2#infinity What is an ∞-category or (∞,1)-category?
          %p
            An (∞,1)-category is a category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched"} enriched
            in (homotopy) spaces.
          %p
            This can be formalized in many different ways. For example, if we
            %a{:href => "#models"} model
            homotopy spaces by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
            with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+weak+equivalences"} simplicial weak equivalences
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dwyer%E2%80%93Kan+equivalences"} Dwyer–Kan equivalences
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+functors"} enriched functors
            that induce a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+weak+equivalence"} simplicial weak equivalence
            on each mapping
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
            (which in this case amounts to replacing each mapping
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
            with its set of connected components), we get an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
          %p
            As one can see from this description, there are substantial
            %a{:href => "#models"} modeling
            issues related to (∞,1)-categories.
          %p
            Other
            %a{:href => "#models"} models
            for (∞,1)-categories include
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+categories"} relative categories
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasicategories"} quasicategories
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Segal+categories"} Segal categories
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+Segal+spaces"} complete Segal spaces
          %p
            All these models are equivalent in a precise sense: we can organize each of them into a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+model+category"} Quillen model category
            so that all resulting
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+categories"} model categories
            are
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+equivalent"} Quillen equivalent
          %p
            The term “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-category"}> ∞-category
            ” has two different meanings: it can either mean (∞,1)-category, or (more recently) it can mean a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasicategory"} quasicategory
            (as used in
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lurie"} Lurie
          %h2#are_quasicategories_modelindependent Are quasicategories model-independent?
          %p
            No, for example, the cardinality of the set of 0-simplices of a small
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasicategory"} quasicategory
            does not have any model-independent meaning, although it is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            invariant.
          %p
            Unfortunately, one often sees the adjective “model-independent” (ab)used to mean that only constructions that are meaningful on the level of underlying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
            are used in a particular argument with
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasicategories"} quasicategories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasicategories"} quasicategories
            in this context: we often write “model-independent” arguments of such type also using
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+categories"} model categories
          %h2#what_is_homotopy_type_theory What is homotopy type theory?
          %p
            See
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory+FAQ"} homotopy type theory FAQ
            for a detailed explanation. Here we only offer a highly impressionistic description.
          %p
            Consider the traditional mathematical setup for (homotopy) spaces
            %a{:href => "#models"} modeled
            by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
            and
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+weak+equivalences"} simplicial weak equivalences
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first-order+logic"} first-order logic
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Zermelo-Fraenkel+axioms"} Zermelo-Fraenkel axioms
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+weak+equivalences"} simplicial weak equivalences
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Homotopy+type+theory"} Homotopy type theory
            replaces this entire stack with a flavor of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            that aims to axiomatize homotopy types (known in the field simply as types) directly, without these intermediate steps.
          %p
            In particular,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first-order+logic"} first-order logic
            is subsumed into
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            in how it chooses to formulate things like existence of (co)products of sets etc., i.e., these concepts are formulated in a categorical way rather than through constructions like
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kuratowski+pairs"} Kuratowski pairs
            etc.
          %p
            More fundamentally, the meaning of equality sign
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo =
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} =
            is altered: now
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo =
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A=B
            (very) roughly means the the space of paths from the point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            to the point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            in whatever ambient space contains the points
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ≠
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\ne B
            and if it is nonempty, we could interpret this as
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo =
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A=B
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            preserves the whole homotopy type of the path space and not just whether it is empty or not. The (Platonic) meaning of the equality sign is thus fundamentally enhanced and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            explains how to manipulate such enhanced equalities.
          %p
            This setup allows us to talk about homotopy types directly, without using models. Furthermore, the analog of weak equivalences in this setting behaves similar to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphisms"} isomorphisms
            (with a caveat that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equality
            is now understood in the enhanced sense). In particular, every equivalence is invertible, unlike (say)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+weak+equivalences"} simplicial weak equivalences
          %p
            Much has been achieved on this path, including a new proof of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Blakers-Massey+theorem"} Blakers-Massey theorem
            that led to new classical consequences after being instantiated in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes"} (∞,1)-toposes
          %p
            However, one serious obstacle to adopting this approach in mainstream
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            is that there is currently (April 2020) no working definition of an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
          %h2#halg What is homotopical algebra?
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Homotopical+Algebra"} Homotopical Algebra
            is the title of a 1967 book by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Quillen"} Daniel Quillen
            that introduced
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+categories"} model categories
          %p
            Ever since then this term has been used to describe research in the area of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+categories"} model categories
          %p
            It is often hard to separate homotopical algebra from homotopy theory. Typically, “homotopical algebra” is used to refer to arguments that use notions from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+categories"} model categories
            rather explicitly, as opposed to (say)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasicategorical"} quasicategorical
            arguments. We do note that setting up the theory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasicategories"} quasicategories
            relies heavily on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+algebra"} homotopical algebra
            and much of Lurie’s
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"} Higher Topos Theory
            is devoted to rather subtle aspects of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+algebra"} homotopical algebra
          %h2#how_are_model_categories_related_to_other_models_of_categories How are model categories related to other models of ∞-categories?
          %p
            If we discard
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofibrations"} cofibrations
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrations"} fibrations
            from the data of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+category"} relative category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+category"} Relative categories
            are one of many equivalent
            %a{:href => "#models"} models
            for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
            has an underlying
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
          %p
            Since the underlying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            only depends on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalences"} weak equivalences
            and not on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofibrations"} cofibrations
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrations"} fibrations
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+categories"} model categories
            with the same
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalences"} weak equivalences
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofibrations"} cofibrations
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrations"} fibrations
            have the same underlying
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
          %p
            Thus, the data of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofibrations"} cofibrations
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrations"} fibrations
            merely enhances the given presentation of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+category"} relative category
            with additional data that helps to organize various computations, e.g., to
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#derived"} derive functors
          %p
            However, the ultimate answer to any computation with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+categories"} model categories
            that makes sense on the level of underlying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
            does not depend on the choice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofibrations"} cofibrations
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrations"} fibrations
            %a{:href => "#models"} model
            certainly does.
          %p
            Another important aspect of the relation between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+categories"} model categories
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
            is that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
            must satisfy additional (co)completeness conditions. In the original definition by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen"} Quillen
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
            must be finitely (co)complete, and its (co)fibrations need not be closed under
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/retracts"} retracts
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            admits finite ∞-(co)limits. In the revised definition by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan"} Kan
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
            must be (co)complete, and its (co)fibrations must be closed under
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/retracts"} retracts
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            admits small ∞-(co)limits. Finally, Hovey’s definition additionally requires factorizations to be functorial. This condition does not seem to alter the class of underlying
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
          %h2#what_is_the_relationship_between_higher_category_theory_and_homotopy_theory What is the relationship between (higher) category theory and homotopy theory?
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} Homotopy theory
            serves as a foundation for much of modern (higher)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-categories"} (∞,n)-categories
            heavily relies on
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#halg"} homotopical algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
            could definitely be seen as being part of both, for example.
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} Homotopy theory
            also somewhat implicitly permeates classical
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
            for diagrams of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            is not the strict
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
          %p
            But this is precisely the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+pullback"} homotopy pullback
            in the category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+categories"} small categories
            equipped with the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+model+structure"} natural model structure
          %h2#derived What is a derived functor?
          %p
            Just as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+categories"} relative categories
            %a{:href => "#models"} model
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+categories"} relative categories
            to
            %a{:href => "#models"} model
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functors"} (∞,1)-functors
          %p
            The easiest way to perform such
            %a{:href => "#models"} modeling
            is to require functors to preserve
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalences"} weak equivalences
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complexes"} chain complexes
            (with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-isomorphisms"} quasi-isomorphisms
            as
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalences"} weak equivalences
          %p
            “Deriving” is a process that
            %a{:href => "#models"} models
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functors"} (∞,1)-functors
            via functors between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+categories"} relative categories
            that need not preserve
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalences"} weak equivalences
          %p
            Multiple nonequivalent definitions of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+functors"} derived functors
            exist in the literature, and functors that can be derived in one of the definitions need not be derivable in another.
          %p
            Typically (in most definitions), deriving a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\colon C\to D
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+categories"} relative categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            involves replacing
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            with
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\circ R
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R\colon C\to C
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/resolution"} resolution
            functor, which is typically require to preserve
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalences"} weak equivalences
            and be itself a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalence"} weak equivalence
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+categories"} relative categories
            (known as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dwyer-Kan+equivalence"} Dwyer-Kan equivalence
            of
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+categories"} relative categories
          %p
            A definition with rather good properties was given recently by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hinich"} Hinich
            and relies on converting a given functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\colon C\to D
            into a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocartesian+fibration"} cocartesian fibration
            (or
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+fibration"} cartesian fibration
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{0\to1\}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\to D
            that preserves
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalences"} weak equivalences
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+functor"} derived functor
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
          %p
            Other (older) definitions involve
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+extensions"} Kan extensions
            along
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localization"} localization
            functors to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+categories"} homotopy categories
          %h2#what_is_the_homotopy_category_of_an_1category_what_are_its_limitations What is the homotopy category of an (∞,1)-category? What are its limitations?
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ho
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ho(C)
            of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is obtained by replacing each of the hom
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} ∞-groupoids
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} hom(x,y)
            with its set of connected components:
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi hom
                      %mrow
                        %mi Ho
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi hom
                      %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} hom_{Ho(C)}(x,y) := \pi_0(hom_C(x,y))
          %p
            This informal description can be formalized in any
            %a{:href => "#models"} model
            of (∞,1)-categories. For instance, for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+categories"} relative categories
            we can formally invert all
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalences"} weak equivalences
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localization"} localization
            being the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
            %em up to homotopy
            instead of strictly simply recovers the underlying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            of a
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+category"} relative category
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
            is a useful tool for extracting invariants from a given
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+category"} relative category
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complexes"} chain complexes
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-isomorphisms"} quasi-isomorphisms
            we can compute
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Ext
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi Ho
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Ch
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ext^n(A,B)=Ho(Ch)(A,B[n]).
          %p
            However, passing to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
            destroys a tremendous amount of information about the underlying
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-limits"} (∞,1)-limits
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-colimits"} (∞,1)-colimits
            from the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/products"} products
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproducts"} coproducts
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
            happens near the end of an argument, where one no longer needs categorical constructions like limits and colimits.
          %h2#what_is_a_triangulated_category_what_are_its_limitations What is a triangulated category? What are its limitations?
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} stable (∞,1)-category
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            that admits finite (∞,1)-(co)limits and such that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+squares"} cartesian squares
            coincide with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocartesian+squares"} cocartesian squares
            and there is a morphism from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
            to the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+object"} initial object
          %p
            As is clear from the definition, being a stable (∞,1)-category is a
            %em property
            of an (∞,1)-category. Roughly speaking, a
            %em triangulated category
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} stable (∞,1)-category
            equipped with the additional data of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+cofiber"} homotopy cofiber
            of any morphism.
          %p
            The above is not quite true. In reality, one axiomatizes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangulated+categories"} triangulated categories
            as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/additive+categories"} additive categories
            with additional data of distinguished triangles (basically,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+cofiber"} homotopy cofiber
            sequences) and suspension functor that satisfies a bunch of axioms. A counterexample due to Muro, Schwede, and Strickland constructs a triangulated category that does not arise as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
            of an (∞,1)-category.
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangulated+category"} Triangulated categories
            are a variation on the theme of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+categories"} homotopy categories
            and suffer from the same set of defects.
          %p
            In particular, computations with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+limits"} homotopy limits
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+colimits"} homotopy colimits
            are extremely difficult or impossible beyond the simplest cases.
          %p
            Much work was put into rectifying this defect of triangulated categories. The resulting constructions are generically known as “enhancements” and include notions such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pretriangulated+dg-categories"} pretriangulated dg-categories
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+derivators"} stable derivators
          %p
            These definitions are rather complicated when compared to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} stable (∞,1)-categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivators"} derivators
            still suffer from the same problems as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+categories"} homotopy categories
          %p
            Thus,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} stable (∞,1)-categories
            due to their simplicity and generality can be reasonably seen as an “ultimate enhancement” of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangulated+categories"} triangulated categories
            that does not suffer from their defects.
          %p
            The most developed
            %a{:href => "#models"} models
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} stable (∞,1)-categories
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+model+categories"} stable model categories
            and stable
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasicategories"} quasicategories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hovey"} Hovey
            in Chapter 7 of his book
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Model+Categories"} Model Categories
            and the latter are treated in Chapter 1 of
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lurie"} Lurie
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Algebra"} Higher Algebra
          %h2#what_is_a_derived_category_what_are_its_limitations What is a derived category? What are its limitations?
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} derived (∞,1)-category
            of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            presented by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+category"} relative category
            whose underlying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            is the category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complexes"} chain complexes
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalences"} weak equivalences
            are
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-isomorphisms"} quasi-isomorphisms
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} derived (∞,1)-category
            recovers the traditional
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+category"} derived category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangulated+category"} triangulated category
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+category"} derived category
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            must not be confused with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category+of+chain+complexes"} homotopy category of chain complexes
            valued in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+category"} relative category
            of
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complexes"} chain complexes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalences"} weak equivalences
            being
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+homotopy+equivalences"} chain homotopy equivalences
            instead of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-isomorphisms"} quasi-isomorphisms
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+category"} derived category
            (as well as the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category+of+chain+complexes"} homotopy category of chain complexes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+categories"} homotopy categories
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangulated+categories"} triangulated categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+limits"} homotopy limits
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+colimits"} homotopy colimits
            beyond the case of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+products"} homotopy products
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+coproducts"} homotopy coproducts
            invariably run into severe difficulties, often insurmountable.
          %p
            One way to resolve this issue is to work with one of the models of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} derived (∞,1)-category
            instead. By far the most popular choice in the literature amounts to working with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+category"} relative category
            described above, often augmented by additional formalisms such as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+categories"} model categories
          %p
            Under additional conditions on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+category"} relative category
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complexes"} chain complexes
            with values in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-isomorphisms"} quasi-isomorphisms
            can be equipped with various
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structures"} model structures
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+model+structure+on+chain+complexes"} projective model structure on chain complexes
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+model+structure+on+chain+complexes"} injective model structure on chain complexes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+resolutions"} projective resolutions
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+resolutions"} injective resolutions
          %p
            Another treatment of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} derived (∞,1)-categories
            can be given using
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+quasicategories"} stable quasicategories
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lurie"} Lurie
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Algebra"} Higher Algebra
